{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FMYDnkuEa6SNBTLWuGbDXi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Reframe gradient from ''monotonic task-type dependence'' to ''benchmark-conditional\n2\t  variance structure with task-type as a partial predictor.'' Claim 1 softened: ''item_id\n3\t  dominance and model×item interaction magnitudes vary across benchmarks, with task\n4\t  complexity as one moderating factor alongside format and difficulty distribution.''\n5\t  Add explicit analysis disentangling format (MC vs free-form vs completion) vs task-type.\n6\t  Claim 2 adds caveat: precision separability confirmed only on MMLU; cross-benchmark\n7\t  precision testing deferred (exp-003 descoped). Claim 3 strengthened — budget variation\n8\t  across benchmarks makes benchmark-specific budgets even more necessary. Hook adjusted\n9\t  to ''variance structure varies across benchmarks, but the gradient is partially\n10\t  confounded with evaluation format — MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) cluster together at\n11\t  ~38-42% item_id, while free-form/completion benchmarks (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH)\n12\t  cluster at ~26-31%, but within-format ordering is weak.'' Contribution 1 reweighted\n13\t  toward model×item quantification (validated in exp-001: 33.6% with out-of-sample\n14\t  D-study Δ=0.004) + benchmark variation. May need ACL ARR October cycle as insurance\n15\t  if HellaSwag anomaly undermines gradient clarity for AAAI reviewers.\n16\t\n17\t  '\n18\timagined_experiments:\n19\t- name: Multi-Benchmark G-Study (Phase 1-2 + Phase 3 partial)\n20\t  plan_id: exp-002\n21\t  description: 'Phase 1 DONE: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC-C/HellaSwag/GSM8K),\n22\t    BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items. Phase\n23\t    2 DONE: 4 models × MATH (5th benchmark), same reduced design. Phase 3 partial:\n24\t    Yi-1.5-9B completed on MMLU+ARC; OLMo-2-7B completed on MMLU. MMLU data reused\n25\t    from exp-001 BF16 subset (no new collection needed). Total new data: ~1.10M records\n26\t    (Phase 1-2) + ~173K (Phase 3 partial) = ~1.27M.\n27\t\n28\t    '\n29\t  expected_results:\n30\t    completion_status: Phase 1-2 done, Phase 3 ~20%\n31\t    total_records_new: 1267200\n32\t    total_records_with_exp001_mmlu_reuse: 1324800\n33\t    n_models_primary: 4\n34\t    n_models_total: 6\n35\t    n_benchmarks: 5\n36\t    gpu_hours_used: 258\n37\t    per_benchmark_accuracy_bf16_mean:\n38\t      MMLU:\n39\t        overall: 0.711\n40\t        Llama: 0.711\n41\t        Gemma: 0.76\n42\t        Qwen: 0.726\n43\t        Mistral: 0.67\n44\t        spread_pp: 9.0\n45\t      ARC:\n46\t        overall: 0.785\n47\t        Llama: 0.79\n48\t        Gemma: 0.848\n49\t        Qwen: 0.818\n50\t        Mistral: 0.682\n51\t        spread_pp: 16.6\n52\t      HellaSwag:\n53\t        overall: 0.815\n54\t        Llama: 0.812\n55\t        Gemma: 0.852\n56\t        Qwen: 0.838\n57\t        Mistral: 0.758\n58\t        spread_pp: 9.4\n59\t      GSM8K:\n60\t        overall: 0.645\n61\t        Llama: 0.615\n62\t        Gemma: 0.68\n63\t        Qwen: 0.78\n64\t        Mistral: 0.485\n65\t        spread_pp: 29.5\n66\t      MATH:\n67\t        overall: 0.283\n68\t        Llama: 0.218\n69\t        Gemma: 0.278\n70\t        Qwen: 0.352\n71\t        Mistral: pending_phase2_tail\n72\t        spread_pp_3model: 13.4\n73\t    cross_model_variance_decomposition:\n74\t      MMLU:\n75\t        item_id: 42.1\n76\t        model_x_item: 33.6\n77\t        model: 3.8\n78\t        prompt_x_item: 3.2\n79\t        seed_x_item: 2.6\n80\t        temperature_x_item: 1.2\n81\t        prompt: 0.5\n82\t        temperature: 0.2\n83\t        seed: 0.1\n84\t        model_x_prompt: 0.3\n85\t        model_x_temperature: 0.1\n86\t        ordering: 0.05\n87\t        residual: 12.25\n88\t      ARC:\n89\t        item_id: 37.8\n90\t        model_x_item: 34.5\n91\t        model: 5.5\n92\t        prompt_x_item: 3.8\n93\t        seed_x_item: 2.3\n94\t        temperature_x_item: 1.6\n95\t        prompt: 0.9\n96\t        temperature: 0.3\n97\t        seed: 0.1\n98\t        model_x_prompt: 0.5\n99\t        model_x_temperature: 0.2\n100\t        ordering: 0.1\n101\t        residual: 12.4\n102\t      HellaSwag:\n103\t        item_id: 29.5\n104\t        model_x_item: 32.8\n105\t        model: 3.2\n106\t        prompt_x_item: 6.2\n107\t        seed_x_item: 4.5\n108\t        temperature_x_item: 2.5\n109\t        prompt: 1.8\n110\t        temperature: 0.5\n111\t        seed: 0.2\n112\t        model_x_prompt: 0.8\n113\t        model_x_temperature: 0.1\n114\t        ordering: 0.1\n115\t        residual: 17.8\n116\t      GSM8K:\n117\t        item_id: 31.2\n118\t        model_x_item: 36.2\n119\t        model: 10.5\n120\t        prompt_x_item: 4.5\n121\t        seed_x_item: 2.8\n122\t        temperature_x_item: 1.8\n123\t        prompt: 1.2\n124\t        temperature: 0.4\n125\t        seed: 0.2\n126\t        model_x_prompt: 0.5\n127\t        model_x_temperature: 0.3\n128\t        ordering: 0.1\n129\t        residual: 10.3\n130\t      MATH_3model:\n131\t        item_id: 25.8\n132\t        model_x_item: 38.5\n133\t        model: 6.5\n134\t        prompt_x_item: 5.8\n135\t        seed_x_item: 4.2\n136\t        temperature_x_item: 2.8\n137\t        prompt: 2.0\n138\t        temperature: 0.8\n139\t        seed: 0.3\n140\t        model_x_prompt: 0.6\n141\t        model_x_temperature: 0.2\n142\t        ordering: 0.1\n143\t        residual: 12.4\n144\t    single_model_item_id_Llama:\n145\t      MMLU: 65.2\n146\t      ARC: 58.0\n147\t      HellaSwag: 50.2\n148\t      GSM8K: 56.5\n149\t      MATH: 48.0\n150\t    single_to_cross_item_id_drop_pp:\n151\t      MMLU: -23.1\n152\t      ARC: -20.2\n153\t      HellaSwag: -20.7\n154\t      GSM8K: -25.3\n155\t      MATH: -22.2\n156\t    item_id_by_hypothesized_complexity_order: MMLU(42.1) > ARC(37.8) > HellaSwag(29.5)\n157\t      < GSM8K(31.2) > MATH(25.8)\n158\t    gradient_break: HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) — HellaSwag drops below GSM8K despite\n159\t      lower task complexity\n160\t    gradient_spread_pp: 16.3\n161\t    gradient_monotonicity_permutation_p: 0.12\n162\t    gradient_monotonicity_interpretation: Directional trend exists but not statistically\n163\t      monotonic at α=0.05\n164\t    format_confound:\n165\t      MC_mean_item_id: 40.0\n166\t      completion_item_id: 29.5\n167\t      free_form_mean_item_id: 28.5\n168\t      MC_vs_nonMC_gap_pp: 11.0\n169\t      within_MC_gradient_pp: 4.3\n170\t      within_free_form_gradient_pp: 5.4\n171\t      within_format_conclusion: weak_or_inconclusive — direction consistent but N=2\n172\t        per format, insufficient for statistical test\n173\t    G_item_cross_model:\n174\t      MMLU: 0.82\n175\t      ARC: 0.783\n176\t      HellaSwag: 0.723\n177\t      GSM8K: 0.736\n178\t      MATH_3model: 0.698\n179\t    D_study_items_G80:\n180\t      MMLU: 414\n181\t      ARC: 452\n182\t      HellaSwag: 535\n183\t      GSM8K: 488\n184\t      MATH: 582\n185\t    D_study_range: 414–582 items\n186\t    per_model_interaction_pct:\n187\t      MMLU:\n188\t        Mistral: 29.25\n189\t        Qwen: 27.11\n190\t        Gemma: 23.95\n191\t        Llama: 19.69\n192\t      ARC:\n193\t        Mistral: 32.8\n194\t        Gemma: 25.5\n195\t        Qwen: 23.2\n196\t        Llama: 18.5\n197\t      HellaSwag:\n198\t        Mistral: 30.5\n199\t        Llama: 26.2\n200\t        Qwen: 24.8\n201\t        Gemma: 18.5\n202\t      GSM8K:\n203\t        Mistral: 35.2\n204\t        Llama: 30.5\n205\t        Gemma: 22.8\n206\t        Qwen: 11.5\n207\t    phase3_5model_mmlu:\n208\t      Yi_accuracy_bf16: 0.652\n209\t      item_id_5model: 41.2\n210\t      model_x_item_5model: 35.0\n211\t      G_item_5model: 0.842\n212\t      D_study_predicted_5model: 0.848\n213\t      D_study_delta: 0.006\n214\t      D_study_validation: 'Second out-of-sample validation: 4-model D-study predicted\n215\t        G=0.848 for 5 models, observed 0.842, Δ=0.006'\n216\t  result_rationale: 'Gradient exists but is noisy because HellaSwag (completion format,\n217\t    29.5%) drops below GSM8K (free-form math, 31.2%) in item_id%. The hypothesized\n218\t    knowledge→reasoning gradient predicts HellaSwag should be intermediate between\n219\t    ARC and GSM8K, but HellaSwag''s completion format drives its item_id lower than\n220\t    expected: the 4-choice completion paradigm introduces more model-specific pattern-matching\n221\t    variance (prompt×item 6.2%, seed×item 4.5%) than MC or free-form formats, compressing\n222\t    the item_id share. GSM8K retains relatively high item_id (31.2%) because math\n223\t    items have very clear universal difficulty structure — trivial arithmetic vs impossible\n224\t    multi-step problems — creating a floor of item-driven variance that persists even\n225\t    cross-model. The format confound is partial: MC benchmarks (MMLU 40.0%, ARC 37.8%)\n226\t    cluster 11pp above non-MC (29.0% mean), but within-format gradients are only 4-5pp\n227\t    with N=2 per format, insufficient to disentangle format from task-type. model×item\n228\t    ranges from 32.8% (HellaSwag) to 38.5% (MATH 3-model), increasing on reasoning\n229\t    benchmarks but not consistently — HellaSwag''s model×item (32.8%) is slightly\n230\t    below MMLU''s (33.6%), breaking the monotonic increase pattern. GSM8K''s model\n231\t    main effect (10.5%) is 2.8× MMLU''s (3.8%), driven by Mistral''s dramatically\n232\t    lower math performance (48.5% vs 78.0% for Qwen) — this large model spread inflates\n233\t    the model component and correspondingly depresses residual (10.3% vs 12.3% on\n234\t    MMLU). MATH (3-model preliminary) shows the lowest item_id (25.8%) and highest\n235\t    model×item (38.5%), consistent with the gradient direction but the 3-model estimate\n236\t    has wider CI than 4-model benchmarks. The D-study validation replicates: 4-model\n237\t    D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models on MMLU, observed 0.842 with Yi added (Δ=0.006),\n238\t    consistent with the exp-001 out-of-sample Δ=0.004. D-study budgets vary from 414\n239\t    (MMLU) to 582 (MATH) items for G≥0.80, but this variation is partially confounded\n240\t    with benchmark design properties (item difficulty distribution, accuracy floor\n241\t    effects) rather than purely task-type. HellaSwag''s high budget (535) despite\n242\t    moderate item_id is driven by its large prompt×item (6.2%) and seed×item (4.5%)\n243\t    which inflate the error term in the G-coefficient denominator.\n244\t\n245\t    '\n246\t  tables:\n247\t  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (4 Models, BF16, Binary)'\n248\t    columns:\n249\t    - Source\n250\t    - MMLU\n251\t    - ARC\n252\t    - HellaSwag\n253\t    - GSM8K\n254\t    - MATH†\n255\t    rows:\n256\t    - - item_id\n257\t      - 42.1%\n258\t      - 37.8%\n259\t      - 29.5%\n260\t      - 31.2%\n261\t      - 25.8%\n262\t    - - model×item\n263\t      - 33.6%\n264\t      - 34.5%\n265\t      - 32.8%\n266\t      - 36.2%\n267\t      - 38.5%\n268\t    - - model\n269\t      - 3.8%\n270\t      - 5.5%\n271\t      - 3.2%\n272\t      - 10.5%\n273\t      - 6.5%\n274\t    - - prompt×item\n275\t      - 3.2%\n276\t      - 3.8%\n277\t      - 6.2%\n278\t      - 4.5%\n279\t      - 5.8%\n280\t    - - seed×item\n281\t      - 2.6%\n282\t      - 2.3%\n283\t      - 4.5%\n284\t      - 2.8%\n285\t      - 4.2%\n286\t    - - temp×item\n287\t      - 1.2%\n288\t      - 1.6%\n289\t      - 2.5%\n290\t      - 1.8%\n291\t      - 2.8%\n292\t    - - residual\n293\t      - 12.3%\n294\t      - 12.4%\n295\t      - 17.8%\n296\t      - 10.3%\n297\t      - 12.4%\n298\t    - - other (main+2-way)\n299\t      - 1.2%\n300\t      - 2.1%\n301\t      - 3.5%\n302\t      - 2.7%\n303\t      - 4.0%\n304\t    note: †MATH uses 3-model preliminary data (Mistral pending). MMLU from exp-001\n305\t      BF16 subset.\n306\t  - name: 'Table: Gradient Summary and Format Confound'\n307\t    columns:\n308\t    - Benchmark\n309\t    - Format\n310\t    - item_id%\n311\t    - model×item%\n312\t    - G_item\n313\t    - D-study G≥0.80\n314\t    rows:\n315\t    - - MMLU\n316\t      - 4-choice MC\n317\t      - 42.1%\n318\t      - 33.6%\n319\t      - '0.820'\n320\t      - 414 items\n321\t    - - ARC-C\n322\t      - 4-choice MC\n323\t      - 37.8%\n324\t      - 34.5%\n325\t      - '0.783'\n326\t      - 452 items\n327\t    - - GSM8K\n328\t      - Free-form\n329\t      - 31.2%\n330\t      - 36.2%\n331\t      - '0.736'\n332\t      - 488 items\n333\t    - - HellaSwag\n334\t      - 4-choice completion\n335\t      - 29.5%\n336\t      - 32.8%\n337\t      - '0.723'\n338\t      - 535 items\n339\t    - - MATH†\n340\t      - Free-form\n341\t      - 25.8%\n342\t      - 38.5%\n343\t      - '0.698'\n344\t      - 582 items\n345\t    note: 'Permutation test for monotonic decrease along hypothesized complexity ordering:\n346\t      p=0.12'\n347\t  - name: 'Table: Single-Model → Cross-Model item_id Drop'\n348\t    columns:\n349\t    - Benchmark\n350\t    - Single (Llama)\n351\t    - Cross (4-model)\n352\t    - Δ (pp)\n353\t    - model×item absorbed\n354\t    rows:\n355\t    - - MMLU\n356\t      - 65.2%\n357\t      - 42.1%\n358\t      - '-23.1'\n359\t      - 33.6%\n360\t    - - ARC\n361\t      - 58.0%\n362\t      - 37.8%\n363\t      - '-20.2'\n364\t      - 34.5%\n365\t    - - HellaSwag\n366\t      - 50.2%\n367\t      - 29.5%\n368\t      - '-20.7'\n369\t      - 32.8%\n370\t    - - GSM8K\n371\t      - 56.5%\n372\t      - 31.2%\n373\t      - '-25.3'\n374\t      - 36.2%\n375\t    - - MATH\n376\t      - 48.0%\n377\t      - 25.8%\n378\t      - '-22.2'\n379\t      - 38.5%\n380\t  - name: 'Table: Per-Model Accuracy by Benchmark (BF16, T=0.0)'\n381\t    columns:\n382\t    - Model\n383\t    - MMLU\n384\t    - ARC\n385\t    - HellaSwag\n386\t    - GSM8K\n387\t    - MATH\n388\t    rows:\n389\t    - - Llama-3.1-8B\n390\t      - '0.711'\n391\t      - '0.790'\n392\t      - '0.812'\n393\t      - '0.615'\n394\t      - '0.218'\n395\t    - - Gemma-2-9B\n396\t      - '0.760'\n397\t      - '0.848'\n398\t      - '0.852'\n399\t      - '0.680'\n400\t      - '0.278'\n401\t    - - Qwen3-8B\n402\t      - '0.726'\n403\t      - '0.818'\n404\t      - '0.838'\n405\t      - '0.780'\n406\t      - '0.352'\n407\t    - - Mistral-7B\n408\t      - '0.670'\n409\t      - '0.682'\n410\t      - '0.758'\n411\t      - '0.485'\n412\t      - —\n413\t    - - Yi-1.5-9B*\n414\t      - '0.652'\n415\t      - '0.735'\n416\t      - —\n417\t      - —\n418\t      - —\n419\t    note: '*Phase 3 partial. OLMo-2-7B completed MMLU only (0.618).'\n420\t  figures_description:\n421\t  - 'Stacked-bar chart (signature figure): 5-benchmark variance decomposition, ordered\n422\t    by item_id descending. item_id in dark blue decreases left→right; model×item in\n423\t    teal increases; prompt×item and seed×item in lighter shades grow for HellaSwag.\n424\t    Format labels (MC / Completion / Free-form) annotated below benchmark names. Clear\n425\t    visual: MC benchmarks have taller blue bars, non-MC have more teal.\n426\t\n427\t    '\n428\t  - 'Scatter plot: item_id% vs hypothesized task-complexity rank, with MC benchmarks\n429\t    as circles and free-form/completion as diamonds. Error bars from bootstrap CIs\n430\t    (exp-004). Dotted line shows hypothesized monotonic decrease; actual data shows\n431\t    the HellaSwag→GSM8K break. Shaded bands show format clusters (MC ~38-42%, non-MC\n432\t    ~26-31%).\n433\t\n434\t    '\n435\t  - 'Heatmap: per-model model×item interaction magnitude across 5 benchmarks, showing\n436\t    Mistral''s interaction increasing dramatically on GSM8K (35.2%) while Qwen''s\n437\t    decreases (11.5%), revealing model-specific benchmark sensitivity.\n438\t\n439\t    '\n440\t  analysis: 'The gradient is the paper''s primary novel finding but requires careful\n441\t    framing in median tier. The 16.3pp spread (MMLU 42.1% → MATH 25.8%) is real but\n442\t    the non-monotonicity at HellaSwag→GSM8K (p=0.12) means the paper cannot claim\n443\t    a ''systematic monotonic gradient.'' Instead: ''benchmark-conditional variance\n444\t    structure with task-type as a partial predictor, confounded with evaluation format.''\n445\t    The MC/non-MC clustering (11pp gap) is the more defensible finding: evaluation\n446\t    format partially determines variance structure independent of task content. Within-format\n447\t    gradient is too weak to draw conclusions (4.3pp within MC, 5.4pp within free-form,\n448\t    N=2 each). model×item as the second-largest source is confirmed across ALL 5 benchmarks\n449\t    (32.8-38.5%), strengthening the exp-001 MMLU finding. D-study validation replicates\n450\t    with Yi (Δ=0.006). Precision separability cannot be tested cross-benchmark (exp-002\n451\t    is BF16-only) — this limits Claim 2 to MMLU evidence from exp-001. MATH 3-model\n452\t    data is preliminary; Mistral completion would likely push MATH model×item above\n453\t    40% given Mistral''s expected ~12% accuracy. For AAAI: the gradient+format confound\n454\t    story is publishable with softened claims, but the HellaSwag anomaly will draw\n455\t    reviewer scrutiny — prepare Discussion paragraph explaining completion format''s\n456\t    effect on variance structure.\n457\t\n458\t    '\n459\t- name: Multi-Hardware G-Study\n460\t  plan_id: exp-003\n461\t  description: 'DESCOPED — hardware TBD. Predictions below are conditional on obtaining\n462\t    a second GPU type (e.g., A100-80GB or H100). Design: 4 models × 2 GPU types ×\n463\t    2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings\n464\t    × 200 MMLU items.\n465\t\n466\t    '\n467\t  expected_results:\n468\t    status: descoped — no second GPU type available\n469\t    total_records_if_run: 1152000\n470\t    hardware_main_binary_pct: 0.3\n471\t    hardware_x_precision_binary_pct: 0.8\n472\t    hardware_x_item_binary_pct: 1.5\n473\t    total_hardware_related_binary_pct: 3.2\n474\t    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n475\t    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.65\n476\t    model_ranking_preserved: true\n477\t    item_id_with_hardware_binary_pct: 41.0\n478\t  result_rationale: 'Hardware effects would be small (total ~3.2% binary) because\n479\t    both GPU types implement identical IEEE 754 operations; differences arise from\n480\t    CUDA kernel scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. FP16 would\n481\t    show 5× larger hardware sensitivity than BF16 (0.65pp vs 0.12pp cross-GPU gap)\n482\t    because FP16''s narrower dynamic range makes accumulation order hardware-dependent.\n483\t    This would strengthen Claim 2 by extending \"precision separability\" to \"hardware\n484\t    separability\" — but the experiment is descoped because infrastructure claim was\n485\t    already narrowed to precision per D003/D007. Without this experiment, Claim 2\n486\t    remains scoped to \"precision on single GPU architecture.\"\n487\t\n488\t    '\n489\t  tables: []\n490\t  figures_description: []\n491\t  analysis: 'Descoped. Paper must explicitly scope Claim 2: \"numerical precision separability\n492\t    on a single GPU architecture (RTX PRO 6000).\" Hardware crossing is natural future\n493\t    work. The BF16≈FP16 finding from exp-001 (Δ=0.23pp) provides indirect evidence\n494\t    that hardware effects would be negligible for BF16, since BF16''s 8-bit exponent\n495\t    provides hardware-independent dynamic range.\n496\t\n497\t    '\n498\t- name: Comprehensive Combined Analysis\n499\t  plan_id: exp-004\n500\t  description: 'Pure analysis (no GPU). Depends on exp-001 (DONE) + exp-002 (Phase\n501\t    1-2 DONE). 17 sub-analyses covering bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, REML comparison,\n502\t    IRT vs G-theory, logprob scoring, fixed-vs-random sensitivity, binary data validation,\n503\t    power analysis, GPU cost, and practitioner toolkit.\n504\t\n505\t    '\n506\t  expected_results:\n507\t    model_as_facet_note: Already incorporated in exp-002 cross-model decomposition\n508\t    bootstrap_CIs_95pct:\n509\t      MMLU:\n510\t        item_id:\n511\t        - 36.0\n512\t        - 48.8\n513\t        model_x_item:\n514\t        - 28.4\n515\t        - 37.9\n516\t        model:\n517\t        - 1.2\n518\t        - 7.5\n519\t      ARC:\n520\t        item_id:\n521\t        - 31.5\n522\t        - 44.2\n523\t        model_x_item:\n524\t        - 29.0\n525\t        - 39.8\n526\t        model:\n527\t        - 2.0\n528\t        - 10.2\n529\t      HellaSwag:\n530\t        item_id:\n531\t        - 23.8\n532\t        - 35.5\n533\t        model_x_item:\n534\t        - 27.5\n535\t        - 38.2\n536\t        model:\n537\t        - 0.8\n538\t        - 6.8\n539\t      GSM8K:\n540\t        item_id:\n541\t        - 25.0\n542\t        - 37.8\n543\t        model_x_item:\n544\t        - 30.5\n545\t        - 41.8\n546\t        model:\n547\t        - 5.2\n548\t        - 16.5\n549\t      MATH_3model:\n550\t        item_id:\n551\t        - 19.2\n552\t        - 33.0\n553\t        model_x_item:\n554\t        - 31.5\n555\t        - 45.2\n556\t        model:\n557\t        - 2.5\n558\t        - 11.5\n559\t    gradient_endpoint_CI_overlap: MMLU lower bound (36.0) overlaps with GSM8K upper\n560\t      bound (37.8) — gradient direction is robust (p=0.003 permutation) but magnitude\n561\t      is imprecise\n562\t    model_x_item_CI_width_note: Wide CIs (±5pp) on model×item reflect only 4 models;\n563\t      8+ models would halve CI width\n564\t    D_study_items_with_CI:\n565\t      MMLU:\n566\t        G80: 414\n567\t        G80_CI:\n568\t        - 350\n569\t        - 490\n570\t        G90: 510\n571\t        G90_CI:\n572\t        - 430\n573\t        - 610\n574\t        G95: 720\n575\t      ARC:\n576\t        G80: 452\n577\t        G80_CI:\n578\t        - 375\n579\t        - 540\n580\t        G90: 555\n581\t        G90_CI:\n582\t        - 465\n583\t        - 665\n584\t        G95: 780\n585\t      HellaSwag:\n586\t        G80: 535\n587\t        G80_CI:\n588\t        - 440\n589\t        - 645\n590\t        G90: 660\n591\t        G90_CI:\n592\t        - 545\n593\t        - 795\n594\t        G95: 930\n595\t      GSM8K:\n596\t        G80: 488\n597\t        G80_CI:\n598\t        - 400\n599\t        - 590\n600\t        G90: 600\n601\t        G90_CI:\n602\t        - 495\n603\t        - 720\n604\t        G95: 845\n605\t      MATH:\n606\t        G80: 582\n607\t        G80_CI:\n608\t        - 470\n609\t        - 710\n610\t        G90: 720\n611\t        G90_CI:\n612\t        - 585\n613\t        - 875\n614\t        G95: 1015\n615\t    D_study_optimal_allocation:\n616\t      MMLU:\n617\t        items: 414\n618\t        prompts: 3\n619\t        seeds: 3\n620\t        temps: 1\n621\t      GSM8K:\n622\t        items: 488\n623\t        prompts: 4\n624\t        seeds: 4\n625\t        temps: 1\n626\t    REML_vs_Henderson:\n627\t      max_diff_pp: 0.45\n628\t      rank_preserved_all_benchmarks: true\n629\t      note: REML-EM (500 iter, Δ<0.003pp convergence) confirms Henderson I on all\n630\t        benchmarks; balanced design makes Henderson I unbiased\n631\t    logprob_scoring:\n632\t      MMLU_item_id_logprob_pct: 55.2\n633\t      MMLU_model_x_item_logprob_pct: 26.8\n634\t      MMLU_G_logprob: 0.875\n635\t      ARC_item_id_logprob_pct: 52.0\n636\t      HellaSwag_item_id_logprob_pct: 42.5\n637\t      GSM8K_logprob: not_applicable — free-form generation, no meaningful per-token\n638\t        logprob\n639\t      logprob_vs_binary_item_id_increase_pp: 10-13pp — continuous logprob captures\n640\t        fine-grained difficulty\n641\t    ranking_stability:\n642\t      model_rank_flip_pct: 8.5\n643\t      rank_flip_driver: temperature=0.7 conditions only\n644\t      precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n645\t      note: Llama-Mistral pair flips on 12% of T=0.7 conditions; all other pairs stable\n646\t    higher_order_interactions:\n647\t      three_way_total_MMLU_pct: 2.8\n648\t      three_way_total_GSM8K_pct: 4.2\n649\t      note: Three-way interactions small but nonzero; model×prompt×item is largest\n650\t        (1.5% MMLU, 2.2% GSM8K)\n651\t    IRT_vs_Gtheory:\n652\t      IRT_recommended_items_MMLU: 116\n653\t      Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU: 414\n654\t      ratio_MMLU: 3.57\n655\t      IRT_recommended_items_ARC: 105\n656\t      Gtheory_recommended_items_ARC: 452\n657\t      ratio_ARC: 4.3\n658\t      IRT_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 98\n659\t      Gtheory_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 535\n660\t      ratio_HellaSwag: 5.46\n661\t      IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K: 120\n662\t      Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K: 488\n663\t      ratio_GSM8K: 4.07\n664\t      ratio_range: 3.6–5.5×\n665\t      G_at_IRT_count_MMLU: 0.529\n666\t      G_at_IRT_count_ARC: 0.498\n667\t      G_at_IRT_count_HellaSwag: 0.412\n668\t      G_at_IRT_count_GSM8K: 0.485\n669\t      gap_explanation: G-theory accounts for prompt×item (3-6%), seed×item (2-5%),\n670\t        and residual (10-18%) that IRT ignores\n671\t    power_analysis:\n672\t      min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n673\t      power_at_2pct: 0.95\n674\t      power_at_1pct: 0.72\n675\t      note: 200 items × ~1150 conditions per benchmark provides >95% power for ≥2%\n676\t        interactions\n677\t    fixed_vs_random:\n678\t      temperature_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 1.2\n679\t      ordering_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 0.3\n680\t      combined_max_change_pp: 1.8\n681\t      qualitative_conclusions_preserved: true\n682\t    binary_data_validation:\n683\t      null_simulation_item_id_pct: 0.08\n684\t      observed_item_id_pct: 42.1\n685\t      ratio: 526.3\n686\t      bernoulli_adjustment_preserves_ranking: true\n687\t    bootstrap_vs_jackknife:\n688\t      CI_width_ratio_range:\n689\t      - 0.94\n690\t      - 1.06\n691\t      independence_violation: negligible\n692\t    design_asymmetry:\n693\t      MMLU_3x3_item_id: 42.1\n694\t      MMLU_2x2_matched_item_id: 42.8\n695\t      diff_pp: 0.7\n696\t      conclusion: Reduced 2×2 design on MMLU reproduces full 3×3 within 1pp\n697\t    G_full_benchmark:\n698\t      MMLU_14042_items: 0.992\n699\t      ARC_2590_items: 0.972\n700\t      HellaSwag_10042_items: 0.988\n701\t      GSM8K_1319_items: 0.938\n702\t      MATH_5000_items: 0.955\n703\t      note: Full benchmarks achieve G>0.93; subsampling to 200 items is the reliability\n704\t        bottleneck\n705\t    gpu_cost:\n706\t      current_practice_per_model_usd: 2–5\n707\t      D_study_recommended_per_model_usd: 80–250\n708\t      cost_multiplier: 20–50×\n709\t  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs reveal that model×item is the most uncertain component\n710\t    (±5pp) because 4 model levels yield only 3 df for variance estimation — this is\n711\t    the fundamental statistical limitation of the current design. Gradient endpoints\n712\t    overlap: MMLU lower CI (36.0%) meets GSM8K upper CI (37.8%), making the gradient\n713\t    directionally robust (p=0.003) but imprecise in magnitude — the paper must say\n714\t    \"10-20pp spread\" not \"16.3pp.\" D-study CIs are correspondingly wide: MMLU G≥0.80\n715\t    requires 350-490 items depending on bootstrap sample. IRT comparison is one of\n716\t    the strongest findings: the 3.6-5.5× item ratio is consistent across all benchmarks\n717\t    and has a clean causal explanation (prompt×item + seed×item + residual dimensions\n718\t    that IRT ignores). HellaSwag has the worst ratio (5.46×) because its high prompt×item\n719\t    and seed×item inflate the G-theory budget disproportionately. REML confirms Henderson\n720\t    I within 0.45pp across all benchmarks — methodological choice is not a concern.\n721\t    Logprob scoring increases item_id to 52-55% because the continuous signal captures\n722\t    fine-grained difficulty (items at 0.55 vs 0.65 logprob difficulty are distinguishable,\n723\t    unlike binary 1/1). Fixed vs random sensitivity shows <2pp change, validating\n724\t    the mixed-model approach. Design asymmetry check (MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2 matched) shows\n725\t    only 0.7pp difference, confirming exp-002''s reduced design is adequate.\n726\t\n727\t    '\n728\t  tables:\n729\t  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Components (All Benchmarks)'\n730\t    columns:\n731\t    - Component\n732\t    - MMLU\n733\t    - ARC\n734\t    - HellaSwag\n735\t    - GSM8K\n736\t    - MATH†\n737\t    rows:\n738\t    - - item_id\n739\t      - '[36.0, 48.8]'\n740\t      - '[31.5, 44.2]'\n741\t      - '[23.8, 35.5]'\n742\t      - '[25.0, 37.8]'\n743\t      - '[19.2, 33.0]'\n744\t    - - model×item\n745\t      - '[28.4, 37.9]'\n746\t      - '[29.0, 39.8]'\n747\t      - '[27.5, 38.2]'\n748\t      - '[30.5, 41.8]'\n749\t      - '[31.5, 45.2]'\n750\t    - - model\n751\t      - '[1.2, 7.5]'\n752\t      - '[2.0, 10.2]'\n753\t      - '[0.8, 6.8]'\n754\t      - '[5.2, 16.5]'\n755\t      - '[2.5, 11.5]'\n756\t    note: 1000 bootstrap resamples (item × condition). CI width driven by 4-model\n757\t      limitation.\n758\t  - name: 'Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements'\n759\t    columns:\n760\t    - Benchmark\n761\t    - IRT items\n762\t    - G-theory items (G≥0.80)\n763\t    - Ratio\n764\t    - G at IRT count\n765\t    rows:\n766\t    - - MMLU\n767\t      - '116'\n768\t      - '414'\n769\t      - 3.57×\n770\t      - '0.529'\n771\t    - - ARC\n772\t      - '105'\n773\t      - '452'\n774\t      - 4.30×\n775\t      - '0.498'\n776\t    - - HellaSwag\n777\t      - '98'\n778\t      - '535'\n779\t      - 5.46×\n780\t      - '0.412'\n781\t    - - GSM8K\n782\t      - '120'\n783\t      - '488'\n784\t      - 4.07×\n785\t      - '0.485'\n786\t    note: G-theory at IRT's recommended item count yields G=0.41-0.53 — far below\n787\t      0.80 threshold\n788\t  - name: 'Table: D-Study Benchmark-Specific Budgets (Practical: T fixed)'\n789\t    columns:\n790\t    - Benchmark\n791\t    - G≥0.80\n792\t    - G≥0.80 CI\n793\t    - G≥0.90\n794\t    - G≥0.90 CI\n795\t    - G≥0.95\n796\t    - Prompts\n797\t    - Seeds\n798\t    rows:\n799\t    - - MMLU\n800\t      - '414'\n801\t      - '[350, 490]'\n802\t      - '510'\n803\t      - '[430, 610]'\n804\t      - '720'\n805\t      - '3'\n806\t      - '3'\n807\t    - - ARC\n808\t      - '452'\n809\t      - '[375, 540]'\n810\t      - '555'\n811\t      - '[465, 665]'\n812\t      - '780'\n813\t      - '3'\n814\t      - '3'\n815\t    - - HellaSwag\n816\t      - '535'\n817\t      - '[440, 645]'\n818\t      - '660'\n819\t      - '[545, 795]'\n820\t      - '930'\n821\t      - '4'\n822\t      - '4'\n823\t    - - GSM8K\n824\t      - '488'\n825\t      - '[400, 590]'\n826\t      - '600'\n827\t      - '[495, 720]'\n828\t      - '845'\n829\t      - '4'\n830\t      - '4'\n831\t    - - MATH\n832\t      - '582'\n833\t      - '[470, 710]'\n834\t      - '720'\n835\t      - '[585, 875]'\n836\t      - '1015'\n837\t      - '4'\n838\t      - '4'\n839\t  - name: 'Table: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, 4-Model)'\n840\t    columns:\n841\t    - Source\n842\t    - Binary\n843\t    - Logprob\n844\t    - Text-exact\n845\t    rows:\n846\t    - - item_id\n847\t      - 42.1%\n848\t      - 55.2%\n849\t      - 3.1%\n850\t    - - model×item\n851\t      - 33.6%\n852\t      - 26.8%\n853\t      - 1.5%\n854\t    - - prompt×item\n855\t      - 3.2%\n856\t      - 2.5%\n857\t      - 34.5%\n858\t    - - residual\n859\t      - 12.3%\n860\t      - 5.8%\n861\t      - 38.2%\n862\t    - - G_item\n863\t      - '0.820'\n864\t      - '0.875'\n865\t      - '0.006'\n866\t  - name: 'Table: Methodological Robustness Summary'\n867\t    columns:\n868\t    - Analysis\n869\t    - Result\n870\t    - Conclusion\n871\t    rows:\n872\t    - - REML vs Henderson I\n873\t      - Max diff 0.45pp, all rankings preserved\n874\t      - Henderson I adequate for balanced design\n875\t    - - Fixed vs random (temp/ordering)\n876\t      - Max change 1.8pp\n877\t      - Conclusions robust to facet classification\n878\t    - - Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\n879\t      - Width ratio 0.94–1.06\n880\t      - Independence violation negligible\n881\t    - - Design asymmetry (3×3 vs 2×2)\n882\t      - Δ = 0.7pp on MMLU\n883\t      - Reduced design adequate\n884\t    - - Null simulation (binary)\n885\t      - Obs/null ratio = 526×\n886\t      - Binary G-theory findings are not artifact\n887\t    - - Full-benchmark G projection\n888\t      - G = 0.94–0.99 at full size\n889\t      - Subsampling is the reliability bottleneck\n890\t  figures_description:\n891\t  - 'Forest plot: Bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id% across 5 benchmarks. Two visual clusters:\n892\t    MC (MMLU, ARC) at 36-48% and non-MC (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) at 19-38%. Endpoint\n893\t    CIs overlap narrowly (MMLU lower vs GSM8K upper), annotated with p=0.003 directional\n894\t    test.\n895\t\n896\t    '\n897\t  - 'D-study projection curves: G vs item count for each benchmark with shaded bootstrap\n898\t    CI bands. Horizontal lines at G=0.80, 0.90. Shows benchmark-specific convergence\n899\t    rates — MMLU crosses 0.80 earliest (~414), MATH latest (~582). IRT recommendation\n900\t    (100-120 items) marked with vertical band showing G=0.41-0.53.\n901\t\n902\t    '\n903\t  - 'Stacked bars: single-model (Llama) vs cross-model (4-model) decomposition for\n904\t    each benchmark. Arrow annotations showing item_id drops (20-25pp) absorbed into\n905\t    model×item. The \"reveal\" figure for Claim 1.\n906\t\n907\t    '\n908\t  - 'Side-by-side: Binary vs logprob vs text-exact scoring metric comparison on MMLU.\n909\t    Three very different stacked bars showing metric-conditional variance structure.\n910\t\n911\t    '\n912\t  - 'Heatmap: model×item interaction strength, 4 models × 5 benchmarks. Mistral shows\n913\t    hot spots on GSM8K (35.2%) and ARC (32.8%); Qwen shows cold spot on GSM8K (11.5%)\n914\t    reflecting superior math capability.\n915\t\n916\t    '\n917\t  analysis: 'exp-004 is the analytical backbone of the paper. Five findings for AAAI\n918\t    framing: (1) IRT vs G-theory ratio of 3.6-5.5× across all benchmarks — this is\n919\t    the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" contribution, now confirmed cross-benchmark.\n920\t    (2) Bootstrap CIs show model×item uncertainty is the limiting factor; the paper\n921\t    must honestly report that 4 models provides only rough magnitude estimates (±5pp).\n922\t    (3) D-study budgets are benchmark-conditional with CIs that overlap substantially\n923\t    (MMLU [350,490] vs GSM8K [400,590]) — the practical message is \"400-600 items\n924\t    for any benchmark\" rather than sharply differentiated budgets. (4) Logprob scoring\n925\t    strengthens the metric-conditional narrative: item_id rises from 42% to 55% under\n926\t    continuous scoring, confirming that binary 0/1 compresses item-level information.\n927\t    (5) All methodological robustness checks pass: REML, fixed-vs-random, bootstrap-vs-jackknife,\n928\t    design asymmetry, null simulation. The paper''s Methodological Defense package\n929\t    is complete.\n930\t\n931\t    '\n932\t- name: 14B Model Scale Extension\n933\t  plan_id: exp-005\n934\t  description: 'P1 priority. Blocked on exp-003 (descoped) — dependency can be removed\n935\t    if run independently at BF16 only. Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU\n936\t    and GSM8K with reduced design: BF16 × 2 temps × 4 prompts × 4 seeds × 2 orderings\n937\t    × 200 items.\n938\t\n939\t    '\n940\t  expected_results:\n941\t    status: planned — not started, blocked on hardware availability\n942\t    qwen3_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.752\n943\t    deepseek_r1_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.735\n944\t    qwen3_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.845\n945\t    deepseek_r1_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.812\n946\t    mmlu_item_id_14b_2model: 38.5\n947\t    mmlu_model_x_item_14b: 28.2\n948\t    gsm8k_item_id_14b_2model: 28.5\n949\t    gsm8k_model_x_item_14b: 35.8\n950\t    variance_hierarchy_preserved: true\n951\t    item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: -3.5\n952\t    deepseek_r1_temp_sensitivity_elevated: true\n953\t    deepseek_r1_temp_x_item_gsm8k: 8.5\n954\t  result_rationale: '14B models would show ~3.5pp lower item_id than 8B because higher\n955\t    capacity reduces floor effects — items impossible for 8B become solvable at 14B,\n956\t    compressing between-item variance. model×item rises because dense (Qwen3-14B)\n957\t    vs reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures create larger model-specific\n958\t    item profiles: DeepSeek excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1''s\n959\t    chain-of-thought traces), Qwen excels on factual knowledge. DeepSeek shows elevated\n960\t    temperature×item on GSM8K (~8.5%) because reasoning- distilled models produce\n961\t    branching chain-of-thought that is more sampling- sensitive. Only 2 models limits\n962\t    statistical power — model×item estimates would have very wide CIs. Position as\n963\t    Discussion-section scale validation, not a primary finding. The practical implication:\n964\t    variance hierarchy is stable at 7-14B, suggesting much larger models (70B+) would\n965\t    be needed to alter the structure.\n966\t\n967\t    '\n968\t  tables:\n969\t  - name: 'Table: 8B vs 14B Variance Structure (MMLU, BF16)'\n970\t    columns:\n971\t    - Source\n972\t    - 8B (4 models)\n973\t    - 14B (2 models)\n974\t    - Δ\n975\t    rows:\n976\t    - - item_id\n977\t      - 42.1%\n978\t      - 38.5%\n979\t      - -3.6pp\n980\t    - - model×item\n981\t      - 33.6%\n982\t      - 28.2%\n983\t      - -5.4pp\n984\t    - - residual\n985\t      - 12.3%\n986\t      - 18.5%\n987\t      - +6.2pp\n988\t    note: 14B model×item appears lower than 8B but is confounded by N=2 models (high\n989\t      residual absorbs signal)\n990\t  figures_description:\n991\t  - 'Paired bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition on MMLU and GSM8K, showing qualitative stability.'\n992\t  analysis: 'Useful for Discussion if compute permits, but not essential for AAAI\n993\t    submission. The key finding (item_id stable, hierarchy preserved) is expected\n994\t    from measurement theory — contribution is confirming the expectation empirically.\n995\t    Low priority given timeline pressure.\n996\t\n997\t    '\n998\t- name: Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)\n999\t  plan_id: exp-006\n1000\t  description: 'P1 priority, independent of exp-003. RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA.\n1001\t    6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4\n1002\t    tasks = 1,728 fine-tuning runs. Validates whether variance hierarchy differs between\n1003\t    inference and fine-tuning.\n1004\t\n1005\t    '\n1006\t  expected_results:\n1007\t    status: planned — not started (200 GPU hours, P1)\n1008\t    total_fine_tuning_runs: 1728\n1009\t    variance_decomposition_summary:\n1010\t      SST2:\n1011\t        dominant: learning_rate (32%)\n1012\t        second: seed (24%)\n1013\t        third: data_shuffle (13%)\n1014\t        seed_x_lr: 11%\n1015\t        gpu: 0.3%\n1016\t        precision: 0.8%\n1017\t        residual: 18.9%\n1018\t      MNLI:\n1019\t        dominant: learning_rate (28%)\n1020\t        second: seed (22%)\n1021\t        third: data_shuffle (16%)\n1022\t        seed_x_lr: 12%\n1023\t        gpu: 0.4%\n1024\t        precision: 0.9%\n1025\t        residual: 20.7%\n1026\t      RTE:\n1027\t        dominant: data_shuffle (26%)\n1028\t        second: learning_rate (20%)\n1029\t        third: seed×lr (15%)\n1030\t        seed: 14%\n1031\t        gpu: 0.5%\n1032\t        precision: 1.0%\n1033\t        residual: 23.5%\n1034\t      COPA:\n1035\t        dominant: data_shuffle (30%)\n1036\t        second: seed (20%)\n1037\t        third: learning_rate (16%)\n1038\t        seed_x_lr: 12%\n1039\t        gpu: 0.4%\n1040\t        precision: 1.2%\n1041\t        residual: 20.4%\n1042\t    hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n1043\t    infrastructure_negligible_both_modes: true\n1044\t    rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n1045\t    rte_bimodal_gap_pp: 4.2\n1046\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance is qualitatively different from inference:\n1047\t    hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items. On large datasets\n1048\t    (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (28-32%) because the optimization landscape\n1049\t    is well-defined and LR determines convergence basin. On small datasets (RTE 277\n1050\t    examples, COPA 400 examples), data shuffle dominates (26-30%) because mini-batch\n1051\t    composition has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single hard\n1052\t    example in early batches steers the model into a different loss basin. seed×LR\n1053\t    interaction (11-15%) creates bimodal accuracy on RTE: two loss basins separated\n1054\t    by ~4pp, consistent with Dodge et al. (2020) and Bui et al. (2025). GPU and precision\n1055\t    effects remain negligible (<1.2%) — the infrastructure-is-negligible finding generalizes\n1056\t    from inference to fine-tuning. Frame as Discussion-section preliminary finding\n1057\t    showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa)\n1058\t    may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning.\n1059\t\n1060\t    '\n1061\t  tables:\n1062\t  - name: 'Table: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy'\n1063\t    columns:\n1064\t    - Rank\n1065\t    - Inference (MMLU)\n1066\t    - FT-Large (SST-2)\n1067\t    - FT-Small (RTE)\n1068\t    rows:\n1069\t    - - '#1'\n1070\t      - item_id (42%)\n1071\t      - learning_rate (32%)\n1072\t      - data_shuffle (26%)\n1073\t    - - '#2'\n1074\t      - model×item (34%)\n1075\t      - seed (24%)\n1076\t      - learning_rate (20%)\n1077\t    - - '#3'\n1078\t      - residual (12%)\n1079\t      - residual (19%)\n1080\t      - seed×lr (15%)\n1081\t    - - Infra total\n1082\t      - precision <0.5%\n1083\t      - gpu+prec 1.1%\n1084\t      - gpu+prec 1.5%\n1085\t  figures_description:\n1086\t  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks), highlighting\n1087\t    the qualitative hierarchy shift. Item_id (blue) dominates inference; learning_rate\n1088\t    (orange) and data_shuffle (green) dominate fine-tuning.\n1089\t\n1090\t    '\n1091\t  analysis: 'Downgraded per Dream #3 directive to Discussion-section preliminary observation.\n1092\t    The finding that infrastructure is negligible in BOTH inference and fine-tuning\n1093\t    strengthens Claim 2, but this is encoder-only and cannot support broad claims.\n1094\t    If AAAI timeline is tight, this experiment is the first to cut. Worth doing for\n1095\t    ACL ARR October if time permits.\n1096\t\n1097\t    '\n1098\t- name: LayerCast Precision Mitigation\n1099\t  plan_id: exp-007\n1100\t  description: 'P2 priority. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional\n1101\t    precision condition on MMLU with 4 models.\n1102\t\n1103\t    '\n1104\t  expected_results:\n1105\t    status: planned — not started (30 GPU hours, P2)\n1106\t    layercast_accuracy: 0.713\n1107\t    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n1108\t    precision_x_item_layercast_pct: 0.02\n1109\t    inference_overhead_pct: 18\n1110\t    g_coefficient_unchanged: true\n1111\t  result_rationale: 'LayerCast reduces precision×item from BF16''s already-negligible\n1112\t    0.04% (post BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis) to ~0.02% — the improvement is\n1113\t    unmeasurable in practice. With BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp from exp-001) and precision\n1114\t    contributing <0.1% of variance, any mitigation strategy provides diminishing returns.\n1115\t    The 18% inference overhead makes LayerCast cost-ineffective. Position as 1-paragraph\n1116\t    appendix material if run; otherwise not worth the GPU hours given AAAI timeline.\n1117\t\n1118\t    '\n1119\t  tables: []\n1120\t  figures_description: []\n1121\t  analysis: 'Lowest priority. Skip for AAAI unless reviewer explicitly asks about\n1122\t    precision mitigation strategies. The BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis from exp-001\n1123\t    already shows precision is a non-issue for practical evaluation (0.0006% variance\n1124\t    after excluding FP32).\n1125\t\n1126\t    '\n1127\t- name: MATH Benchmark\n1128\t  plan_id: exp-008\n1129\t  description: Merged into exp-002 Phase 2. All results included in exp-002 predictions\n1130\t    above.\n1131\t  expected_results:\n1132\t    status: merged_into_exp002\n1133\t    see: exp-002 MATH results (3-model preliminary)\n1134\t  result_rationale: No standalone experiment — MATH data collected as part of exp-002\n1135\t    Phase 2.\n1136\t  tables: []\n1137\t  figures_description: []\n1138\t  analysis: See exp-002.\n1139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e048c7cb-c26e-4a6a-9302-1c61fd6549ca","uuid":"77dda0fd-8a06-409a-9182-081c7ea0d752","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:43:11.829Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Reframe gradient from ''monotonic task-type dependence'' to ''benchmark-conditional\n  variance structure with task-type as a partial predictor.'' Claim 1 softened: ''item_id\n  dominance and model×item interaction magnitudes vary across benchmarks, with task\n  complexity as one moderating factor alongside format and difficulty distribution.''\n  Add explicit analysis disentangling format (MC vs free-form vs completion) vs task-type.\n  Claim 2 adds caveat: precision separability confirmed only on MMLU; cross-benchmark\n  precision testing deferred (exp-003 descoped). Claim 3 strengthened — budget variation\n  across benchmarks makes benchmark-specific budgets even more necessary. Hook adjusted\n  to ''variance structure varies across benchmarks, but the gradient is partially\n  confounded with evaluation format — MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) cluster together at\n  ~38-42% item_id, while free-form/completion benchmarks (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH)\n  cluster at ~26-31%, but within-format ordering is weak.'' Contribution 1 reweighted\n  toward model×item quantification (validated in exp-001: 33.6% with out-of-sample\n  D-study Δ=0.004) + benchmark variation. May need ACL ARR October cycle as insurance\n  if HellaSwag anomaly undermines gradient clarity for AAAI reviewers.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Multi-Benchmark G-Study (Phase 1-2 + Phase 3 partial)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: 'Phase 1 DONE: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC-C/HellaSwag/GSM8K),\n    BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items. Phase\n    2 DONE: 4 models × MATH (5th benchmark), same reduced design. Phase 3 partial:\n    Yi-1.5-9B completed on MMLU+ARC; OLMo-2-7B completed on MMLU. MMLU data reused\n    from exp-001 BF16 subset (no new collection needed). Total new data: ~1.10M records\n    (Phase 1-2) + ~173K (Phase 3 partial) = ~1.27M.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    completion_status: Phase 1-2 done, Phase 3 ~20%\n    total_records_new: 1267200\n    total_records_with_exp001_mmlu_reuse: 1324800\n    n_models_primary: 4\n    n_models_total: 6\n    n_benchmarks: 5\n    gpu_hours_used: 258\n    per_benchmark_accuracy_bf16_mean:\n      MMLU:\n        overall: 0.711\n        Llama: 0.711\n        Gemma: 0.76\n        Qwen: 0.726\n        Mistral: 0.67\n        spread_pp: 9.0\n      ARC:\n        overall: 0.785\n        Llama: 0.79\n        Gemma: 0.848\n        Qwen: 0.818\n        Mistral: 0.682\n        spread_pp: 16.6\n      HellaSwag:\n        overall: 0.815\n        Llama: 0.812\n        Gemma: 0.852\n        Qwen: 0.838\n        Mistral: 0.758\n        spread_pp: 9.4\n      GSM8K:\n        overall: 0.645\n        Llama: 0.615\n        Gemma: 0.68\n        Qwen: 0.78\n        Mistral: 0.485\n        spread_pp: 29.5\n      MATH:\n        overall: 0.283\n        Llama: 0.218\n        Gemma: 0.278\n        Qwen: 0.352\n        Mistral: pending_phase2_tail\n        spread_pp_3model: 13.4\n    cross_model_variance_decomposition:\n      MMLU:\n        item_id: 42.1\n        model_x_item: 33.6\n        model: 3.8\n        prompt_x_item: 3.2\n        seed_x_item: 2.6\n        temperature_x_item: 1.2\n        prompt: 0.5\n        temperature: 0.2\n        seed: 0.1\n        model_x_prompt: 0.3\n        model_x_temperature: 0.1\n        ordering: 0.05\n        residual: 12.25\n      ARC:\n        item_id: 37.8\n        model_x_item: 34.5\n        model: 5.5\n        prompt_x_item: 3.8\n        seed_x_item: 2.3\n        temperature_x_item: 1.6\n        prompt: 0.9\n        temperature: 0.3\n        seed: 0.1\n        model_x_prompt: 0.5\n        model_x_temperature: 0.2\n        ordering: 0.1\n        residual: 12.4\n      HellaSwag:\n        item_id: 29.5\n        model_x_item: 32.8\n        model: 3.2\n        prompt_x_item: 6.2\n        seed_x_item: 4.5\n        temperature_x_item: 2.5\n        prompt: 1.8\n        temperature: 0.5\n        seed: 0.2\n        model_x_prompt: 0.8\n        model_x_temperature: 0.1\n        ordering: 0.1\n        residual: 17.8\n      GSM8K:\n        item_id: 31.2\n        model_x_item: 36.2\n        model: 10.5\n        prompt_x_item: 4.5\n        seed_x_item: 2.8\n        temperature_x_item: 1.8\n        prompt: 1.2\n        temperature: 0.4\n        seed: 0.2\n        model_x_prompt: 0.5\n        model_x_temperature: 0.3\n        ordering: 0.1\n        residual: 10.3\n      MATH_3model:\n        item_id: 25.8\n        model_x_item: 38.5\n        model: 6.5\n        prompt_x_item: 5.8\n        seed_x_item: 4.2\n        temperature_x_item: 2.8\n        prompt: 2.0\n        temperature: 0.8\n        seed: 0.3\n        model_x_prompt: 0.6\n        model_x_temperature: 0.2\n        ordering: 0.1\n        residual: 12.4\n    single_model_item_id_Llama:\n      MMLU: 65.2\n      ARC: 58.0\n      HellaSwag: 50.2\n      GSM8K: 56.5\n      MATH: 48.0\n    single_to_cross_item_id_drop_pp:\n      MMLU: -23.1\n      ARC: -20.2\n      HellaSwag: -20.7\n      GSM8K: -25.3\n      MATH: -22.2\n    item_id_by_hypothesized_complexity_order: MMLU(42.1) > ARC(37.8) > HellaSwag(29.5)\n      < GSM8K(31.2) > MATH(25.8)\n    gradient_break: HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) — HellaSwag drops below GSM8K despite\n      lower task complexity\n    gradient_spread_pp: 16.3\n    gradient_monotonicity_permutation_p: 0.12\n    gradient_monotonicity_interpretation: Directional trend exists but not statistically\n      monotonic at α=0.05\n    format_confound:\n      MC_mean_item_id: 40.0\n      completion_item_id: 29.5\n      free_form_mean_item_id: 28.5\n      MC_vs_nonMC_gap_pp: 11.0\n      within_MC_gradient_pp: 4.3\n      within_free_form_gradient_pp: 5.4\n      within_format_conclusion: weak_or_inconclusive — direction consistent but N=2\n        per format, insufficient for statistical test\n    G_item_cross_model:\n      MMLU: 0.82\n      ARC: 0.783\n      HellaSwag: 0.723\n      GSM8K: 0.736\n      MATH_3model: 0.698\n    D_study_items_G80:\n      MMLU: 414\n      ARC: 452\n      HellaSwag: 535\n      GSM8K: 488\n      MATH: 582\n    D_study_range: 414–582 items\n    per_model_interaction_pct:\n      MMLU:\n        Mistral: 29.25\n        Qwen: 27.11\n        Gemma: 23.95\n        Llama: 19.69\n      ARC:\n        Mistral: 32.8\n        Gemma: 25.5\n        Qwen: 23.2\n        Llama: 18.5\n      HellaSwag:\n        Mistral: 30.5\n        Llama: 26.2\n        Qwen: 24.8\n        Gemma: 18.5\n      GSM8K:\n        Mistral: 35.2\n        Llama: 30.5\n        Gemma: 22.8\n        Qwen: 11.5\n    phase3_5model_mmlu:\n      Yi_accuracy_bf16: 0.652\n      item_id_5model: 41.2\n      model_x_item_5model: 35.0\n      G_item_5model: 0.842\n      D_study_predicted_5model: 0.848\n      D_study_delta: 0.006\n      D_study_validation: 'Second out-of-sample validation: 4-model D-study predicted\n        G=0.848 for 5 models, observed 0.842, Δ=0.006'\n  result_rationale: 'Gradient exists but is noisy because HellaSwag (completion format,\n    29.5%) drops below GSM8K (free-form math, 31.2%) in item_id%. The hypothesized\n    knowledge→reasoning gradient predicts HellaSwag should be intermediate between\n    ARC and GSM8K, but HellaSwag''s completion format drives its item_id lower than\n    expected: the 4-choice completion paradigm introduces more model-specific pattern-matching\n    variance (prompt×item 6.2%, seed×item 4.5%) than MC or free-form formats, compressing\n    the item_id share. GSM8K retains relatively high item_id (31.2%) because math\n    items have very clear universal difficulty structure — trivial arithmetic vs impossible\n    multi-step problems — creating a floor of item-driven variance that persists even\n    cross-model. The format confound is partial: MC benchmarks (MMLU 40.0%, ARC 37.8%)\n    cluster 11pp above non-MC (29.0% mean), but within-format gradients are only 4-5pp\n    with N=2 per format, insufficient to disentangle format from task-type. model×item\n    ranges from 32.8% (HellaSwag) to 38.5% (MATH 3-model), increasing on reasoning\n    benchmarks but not consistently — HellaSwag''s model×item (32.8%) is slightly\n    below MMLU''s (33.6%), breaking the monotonic increase pattern. GSM8K''s model\n    main effect (10.5%) is 2.8× MMLU''s (3.8%), driven by Mistral''s dramatically\n    lower math performance (48.5% vs 78.0% for Qwen) — this large model spread inflates\n    the model component and correspondingly depresses residual (10.3% vs 12.3% on\n    MMLU). MATH (3-model preliminary) shows the lowest item_id (25.8%) and highest\n    model×item (38.5%), consistent with the gradient direction but the 3-model estimate\n    has wider CI than 4-model benchmarks. The D-study validation replicates: 4-model\n    D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models on MMLU, observed 0.842 with Yi added (Δ=0.006),\n    consistent with the exp-001 out-of-sample Δ=0.004. D-study budgets vary from 414\n    (MMLU) to 582 (MATH) items for G≥0.80, but this variation is partially confounded\n    with benchmark design properties (item difficulty distribution, accuracy floor\n    effects) rather than purely task-type. HellaSwag''s high budget (535) despite\n    moderate item_id is driven by its large prompt×item (6.2%) and seed×item (4.5%)\n    which inflate the error term in the G-coefficient denominator.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (4 Models, BF16, Binary)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - MMLU\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    - GSM8K\n    - MATH†\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 42.1%\n      - 37.8%\n      - 29.5%\n      - 31.2%\n      - 25.8%\n    - - model×item\n      - 33.6%\n      - 34.5%\n      - 32.8%\n      - 36.2%\n      - 38.5%\n    - - model\n      - 3.8%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 3.2%\n      - 10.5%\n      - 6.5%\n    - - prompt×item\n      - 3.2%\n      - 3.8%\n      - 6.2%\n      - 4.5%\n      - 5.8%\n    - - seed×item\n      - 2.6%\n      - 2.3%\n      - 4.5%\n      - 2.8%\n      - 4.2%\n    - - temp×item\n      - 1.2%\n      - 1.6%\n      - 2.5%\n      - 1.8%\n      - 2.8%\n    - - residual\n      - 12.3%\n      - 12.4%\n      - 17.8%\n      - 10.3%\n      - 12.4%\n    - - other (main+2-way)\n      - 1.2%\n      - 2.1%\n      - 3.5%\n      - 2.7%\n      - 4.0%\n    note: †MATH uses 3-model preliminary data (Mistral pending). MMLU from exp-001\n      BF16 subset.\n  - name: 'Table: Gradient Summary and Format Confound'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Format\n    - item_id%\n    - model×item%\n    - G_item\n    - D-study G≥0.80\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 4-choice MC\n      - 42.1%\n      - 33.6%\n      - '0.820'\n      - 414 items\n    - - ARC-C\n      - 4-choice MC\n      - 37.8%\n      - 34.5%\n      - '0.783'\n      - 452 items\n    - - GSM8K\n      - Free-form\n      - 31.2%\n      - 36.2%\n      - '0.736'\n      - 488 items\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - 4-choice completion\n      - 29.5%\n      - 32.8%\n      - '0.723'\n      - 535 items\n    - - MATH†\n      - Free-form\n      - 25.8%\n      - 38.5%\n      - '0.698'\n      - 582 items\n    note: 'Permutation test for monotonic decrease along hypothesized complexity ordering:\n      p=0.12'\n  - name: 'Table: Single-Model → Cross-Model item_id Drop'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Single (Llama)\n    - Cross (4-model)\n    - Δ (pp)\n    - model×item absorbed\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 65.2%\n      - 42.1%\n      - '-23.1'\n      - 33.6%\n    - - ARC\n      - 58.0%\n      - 37.8%\n      - '-20.2'\n      - 34.5%\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - 50.2%\n      - 29.5%\n      - '-20.7'\n      - 32.8%\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 56.5%\n      - 31.2%\n      - '-25.3'\n      - 36.2%\n    - - MATH\n      - 48.0%\n      - 25.8%\n      - '-22.2'\n      - 38.5%\n  - name: 'Table: Per-Model Accuracy by Benchmark (BF16, T=0.0)'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - MMLU\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    - GSM8K\n    - MATH\n    rows:\n    - - Llama-3.1-8B\n      - '0.711'\n      - '0.790'\n      - '0.812'\n      - '0.615'\n      - '0.218'\n    - - Gemma-2-9B\n      - '0.760'\n      - '0.848'\n      - '0.852'\n      - '0.680'\n      - '0.278'\n    - - Qwen3-8B\n      - '0.726'\n      - '0.818'\n      - '0.838'\n      - '0.780'\n      - '0.352'\n    - - Mistral-7B\n      - '0.670'\n      - '0.682'\n      - '0.758'\n      - '0.485'\n      - —\n    - - Yi-1.5-9B*\n      - '0.652'\n      - '0.735'\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n    note: '*Phase 3 partial. OLMo-2-7B completed MMLU only (0.618).'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked-bar chart (signature figure): 5-benchmark variance decomposition, ordered\n    by item_id descending. item_id in dark blue decreases left→right; model×item in\n    teal increases; prompt×item and seed×item in lighter shades grow for HellaSwag.\n    Format labels (MC / Completion / Free-form) annotated below benchmark names. Clear\n    visual: MC benchmarks have taller blue bars, non-MC have more teal.\n\n    '\n  - 'Scatter plot: item_id% vs hypothesized task-complexity rank, with MC benchmarks\n    as circles and free-form/completion as diamonds. Error bars from bootstrap CIs\n    (exp-004). Dotted line shows hypothesized monotonic decrease; actual data shows\n    the HellaSwag→GSM8K break. Shaded bands show format clusters (MC ~38-42%, non-MC\n    ~26-31%).\n\n    '\n  - 'Heatmap: per-model model×item interaction magnitude across 5 benchmarks, showing\n    Mistral''s interaction increasing dramatically on GSM8K (35.2%) while Qwen''s\n    decreases (11.5%), revealing model-specific benchmark sensitivity.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The gradient is the paper''s primary novel finding but requires careful\n    framing in median tier. The 16.3pp spread (MMLU 42.1% → MATH 25.8%) is real but\n    the non-monotonicity at HellaSwag→GSM8K (p=0.12) means the paper cannot claim\n    a ''systematic monotonic gradient.'' Instead: ''benchmark-conditional variance\n    structure with task-type as a partial predictor, confounded with evaluation format.''\n    The MC/non-MC clustering (11pp gap) is the more defensible finding: evaluation\n    format partially determines variance structure independent of task content. Within-format\n    gradient is too weak to draw conclusions (4.3pp within MC, 5.4pp within free-form,\n    N=2 each). model×item as the second-largest source is confirmed across ALL 5 benchmarks\n    (32.8-38.5%), strengthening the exp-001 MMLU finding. D-study validation replicates\n    with Yi (Δ=0.006). Precision separability cannot be tested cross-benchmark (exp-002\n    is BF16-only) — this limits Claim 2 to MMLU evidence from exp-001. MATH 3-model\n    data is preliminary; Mistral completion would likely push MATH model×item above\n    40% given Mistral''s expected ~12% accuracy. For AAAI: the gradient+format confound\n    story is publishable with softened claims, but the HellaSwag anomaly will draw\n    reviewer scrutiny — prepare Discussion paragraph explaining completion format''s\n    effect on variance structure.\n\n    '\n- name: Multi-Hardware G-Study\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'DESCOPED — hardware TBD. Predictions below are conditional on obtaining\n    a second GPU type (e.g., A100-80GB or H100). Design: 4 models × 2 GPU types ×\n    2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings\n    × 200 MMLU items.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: descoped — no second GPU type available\n    total_records_if_run: 1152000\n    hardware_main_binary_pct: 0.3\n    hardware_x_precision_binary_pct: 0.8\n    hardware_x_item_binary_pct: 1.5\n    total_hardware_related_binary_pct: 3.2\n    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.65\n    model_ranking_preserved: true\n    item_id_with_hardware_binary_pct: 41.0\n  result_rationale: 'Hardware effects would be small (total ~3.2% binary) because\n    both GPU types implement identical IEEE 754 operations; differences arise from\n    CUDA kernel scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. FP16 would\n    show 5× larger hardware sensitivity than BF16 (0.65pp vs 0.12pp cross-GPU gap)\n    because FP16''s narrower dynamic range makes accumulation order hardware-dependent.\n    This would strengthen Claim 2 by extending \"precision separability\" to \"hardware\n    separability\" — but the experiment is descoped because infrastructure claim was\n    already narrowed to precision per D003/D007. Without this experiment, Claim 2\n    remains scoped to \"precision on single GPU architecture.\"\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Descoped. Paper must explicitly scope Claim 2: \"numerical precision separability\n    on a single GPU architecture (RTX PRO 6000).\" Hardware crossing is natural future\n    work. The BF16≈FP16 finding from exp-001 (Δ=0.23pp) provides indirect evidence\n    that hardware effects would be negligible for BF16, since BF16''s 8-bit exponent\n    provides hardware-independent dynamic range.\n\n    '\n- name: Comprehensive Combined Analysis\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Pure analysis (no GPU). Depends on exp-001 (DONE) + exp-002 (Phase\n    1-2 DONE). 17 sub-analyses covering bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, REML comparison,\n    IRT vs G-theory, logprob scoring, fixed-vs-random sensitivity, binary data validation,\n    power analysis, GPU cost, and practitioner toolkit.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    model_as_facet_note: Already incorporated in exp-002 cross-model decomposition\n    bootstrap_CIs_95pct:\n      MMLU:\n        item_id:\n        - 36.0\n        - 48.8\n        model_x_item:\n        - 28.4\n        - 37.9\n        model:\n        - 1.2\n        - 7.5\n      ARC:\n        item_id:\n        - 31.5\n        - 44.2\n        model_x_item:\n        - 29.0\n        - 39.8\n        model:\n        - 2.0\n        - 10.2\n      HellaSwag:\n        item_id:\n        - 23.8\n        - 35.5\n        model_x_item:\n        - 27.5\n        - 38.2\n        model:\n        - 0.8\n        - 6.8\n      GSM8K:\n        item_id:\n        - 25.0\n        - 37.8\n        model_x_item:\n        - 30.5\n        - 41.8\n        model:\n        - 5.2\n        - 16.5\n      MATH_3model:\n        item_id:\n        - 19.2\n        - 33.0\n        model_x_item:\n        - 31.5\n        - 45.2\n        model:\n        - 2.5\n        - 11.5\n    gradient_endpoint_CI_overlap: MMLU lower bound (36.0) overlaps with GSM8K upper\n      bound (37.8) — gradient direction is robust (p=0.003 permutation) but magnitude\n      is imprecise\n    model_x_item_CI_width_note: Wide CIs (±5pp) on model×item reflect only 4 models;\n      8+ models would halve CI width\n    D_study_items_with_CI:\n      MMLU:\n        G80: 414\n        G80_CI:\n        - 350\n        - 490\n        G90: 510\n        G90_CI:\n        - 430\n        - 610\n        G95: 720\n      ARC:\n        G80: 452\n        G80_CI:\n        - 375\n        - 540\n        G90: 555\n        G90_CI:\n        - 465\n        - 665\n        G95: 780\n      HellaSwag:\n        G80: 535\n        G80_CI:\n        - 440\n        - 645\n        G90: 660\n        G90_CI:\n        - 545\n        - 795\n        G95: 930\n      GSM8K:\n        G80: 488\n        G80_CI:\n        - 400\n        - 590\n        G90: 600\n        G90_CI:\n        - 495\n        - 720\n        G95: 845\n      MATH:\n        G80: 582\n        G80_CI:\n        - 470\n        - 710\n        G90: 720\n        G90_CI:\n        - 585\n        - 875\n        G95: 1015\n    D_study_optimal_allocation:\n      MMLU:\n        items: 414\n        prompts: 3\n        seeds: 3\n        temps: 1\n      GSM8K:\n        items: 488\n        prompts: 4\n        seeds: 4\n        temps: 1\n    REML_vs_Henderson:\n      max_diff_pp: 0.45\n      rank_preserved_all_benchmarks: true\n      note: REML-EM (500 iter, Δ<0.003pp convergence) confirms Henderson I on all\n        benchmarks; balanced design makes Henderson I unbiased\n    logprob_scoring:\n      MMLU_item_id_logprob_pct: 55.2\n      MMLU_model_x_item_logprob_pct: 26.8\n      MMLU_G_logprob: 0.875\n      ARC_item_id_logprob_pct: 52.0\n      HellaSwag_item_id_logprob_pct: 42.5\n      GSM8K_logprob: not_applicable — free-form generation, no meaningful per-token\n        logprob\n      logprob_vs_binary_item_id_increase_pp: 10-13pp — continuous logprob captures\n        fine-grained difficulty\n    ranking_stability:\n      model_rank_flip_pct: 8.5\n      rank_flip_driver: temperature=0.7 conditions only\n      precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n      note: Llama-Mistral pair flips on 12% of T=0.7 conditions; all other pairs stable\n    higher_order_interactions:\n      three_way_total_MMLU_pct: 2.8\n      three_way_total_GSM8K_pct: 4.2\n      note: Three-way interactions small but nonzero; model×prompt×item is largest\n        (1.5% MMLU, 2.2% GSM8K)\n    IRT_vs_Gtheory:\n      IRT_recommended_items_MMLU: 116\n      Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU: 414\n      ratio_MMLU: 3.57\n      IRT_recommended_items_ARC: 105\n      Gtheory_recommended_items_ARC: 452\n      ratio_ARC: 4.3\n      IRT_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 98\n      Gtheory_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 535\n      ratio_HellaSwag: 5.46\n      IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K: 120\n      Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K: 488\n      ratio_GSM8K: 4.07\n      ratio_range: 3.6–5.5×\n      G_at_IRT_count_MMLU: 0.529\n      G_at_IRT_count_ARC: 0.498\n      G_at_IRT_count_HellaSwag: 0.412\n      G_at_IRT_count_GSM8K: 0.485\n      gap_explanation: G-theory accounts for prompt×item (3-6%), seed×item (2-5%),\n        and residual (10-18%) that IRT ignores\n    power_analysis:\n      min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n      power_at_2pct: 0.95\n      power_at_1pct: 0.72\n      note: 200 items × ~1150 conditions per benchmark provides >95% power for ≥2%\n        interactions\n    fixed_vs_random:\n      temperature_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 1.2\n      ordering_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 0.3\n      combined_max_change_pp: 1.8\n      qualitative_conclusions_preserved: true\n    binary_data_validation:\n      null_simulation_item_id_pct: 0.08\n      observed_item_id_pct: 42.1\n      ratio: 526.3\n      bernoulli_adjustment_preserves_ranking: true\n    bootstrap_vs_jackknife:\n      CI_width_ratio_range:\n      - 0.94\n      - 1.06\n      independence_violation: negligible\n    design_asymmetry:\n      MMLU_3x3_item_id: 42.1\n      MMLU_2x2_matched_item_id: 42.8\n      diff_pp: 0.7\n      conclusion: Reduced 2×2 design on MMLU reproduces full 3×3 within 1pp\n    G_full_benchmark:\n      MMLU_14042_items: 0.992\n      ARC_2590_items: 0.972\n      HellaSwag_10042_items: 0.988\n      GSM8K_1319_items: 0.938\n      MATH_5000_items: 0.955\n      note: Full benchmarks achieve G>0.93; subsampling to 200 items is the reliability\n        bottleneck\n    gpu_cost:\n      current_practice_per_model_usd: 2–5\n      D_study_recommended_per_model_usd: 80–250\n      cost_multiplier: 20–50×\n  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs reveal that model×item is the most uncertain component\n    (±5pp) because 4 model levels yield only 3 df for variance estimation — this is\n    the fundamental statistical limitation of the current design. Gradient endpoints\n    overlap: MMLU lower CI (36.0%) meets GSM8K upper CI (37.8%), making the gradient\n    directionally robust (p=0.003) but imprecise in magnitude — the paper must say\n    \"10-20pp spread\" not \"16.3pp.\" D-study CIs are correspondingly wide: MMLU G≥0.80\n    requires 350-490 items depending on bootstrap sample. IRT comparison is one of\n    the strongest findings: the 3.6-5.5× item ratio is consistent across all benchmarks\n    and has a clean causal explanation (prompt×item + seed×item + residual dimensions\n    that IRT ignores). HellaSwag has the worst ratio (5.46×) because its high prompt×item\n    and seed×item inflate the G-theory budget disproportionately. REML confirms Henderson\n    I within 0.45pp across all benchmarks — methodological choice is not a concern.\n    Logprob scoring increases item_id to 52-55% because the continuous signal captures\n    fine-grained difficulty (items at 0.55 vs 0.65 logprob difficulty are distinguishable,\n    unlike binary 1/1). Fixed vs random sensitivity shows <2pp change, validating\n    the mixed-model approach. Design asymmetry check (MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2 matched) shows\n    only 0.7pp difference, confirming exp-002''s reduced design is adequate.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Components (All Benchmarks)'\n    columns:\n    - Component\n    - MMLU\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    - GSM8K\n    - MATH†\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - '[36.0, 48.8]'\n      - '[31.5, 44.2]'\n      - '[23.8, 35.5]'\n      - '[25.0, 37.8]'\n      - '[19.2, 33.0]'\n    - - model×item\n      - '[28.4, 37.9]'\n      - '[29.0, 39.8]'\n      - '[27.5, 38.2]'\n      - '[30.5, 41.8]'\n      - '[31.5, 45.2]'\n    - - model\n      - '[1.2, 7.5]'\n      - '[2.0, 10.2]'\n      - '[0.8, 6.8]'\n      - '[5.2, 16.5]'\n      - '[2.5, 11.5]'\n    note: 1000 bootstrap resamples (item × condition). CI width driven by 4-model\n      limitation.\n  - name: 'Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - IRT items\n    - G-theory items (G≥0.80)\n    - Ratio\n    - G at IRT count\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - '116'\n      - '414'\n      - 3.57×\n      - '0.529'\n    - - ARC\n      - '105'\n      - '452'\n      - 4.30×\n      - '0.498'\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - '98'\n      - '535'\n      - 5.46×\n      - '0.412'\n    - - GSM8K\n      - '120'\n      - '488'\n      - 4.07×\n      - '0.485'\n    note: G-theory at IRT's recommended item count yields G=0.41-0.53 — far below\n      0.80 threshold\n  - name: 'Table: D-Study Benchmark-Specific Budgets (Practical: T fixed)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - G≥0.80\n    - G≥0.80 CI\n    - G≥0.90\n    - G≥0.90 CI\n    - G≥0.95\n    - Prompts\n    - Seeds\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - '414'\n      - '[350, 490]'\n      - '510'\n      - '[430, 610]'\n      - '720'\n      - '3'\n      - '3'\n    - - ARC\n      - '452'\n      - '[375, 540]'\n      - '555'\n      - '[465, 665]'\n      - '780'\n      - '3'\n      - '3'\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - '535'\n      - '[440, 645]'\n      - '660'\n      - '[545, 795]'\n      - '930'\n      - '4'\n      - '4'\n    - - GSM8K\n      - '488'\n      - '[400, 590]'\n      - '600'\n      - '[495, 720]'\n      - '845'\n      - '4'\n      - '4'\n    - - MATH\n      - '582'\n      - '[470, 710]'\n      - '720'\n      - '[585, 875]'\n      - '1015'\n      - '4'\n      - '4'\n  - name: 'Table: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, 4-Model)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Binary\n    - Logprob\n    - Text-exact\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 42.1%\n      - 55.2%\n      - 3.1%\n    - - model×item\n      - 33.6%\n      - 26.8%\n      - 1.5%\n    - - prompt×item\n      - 3.2%\n      - 2.5%\n      - 34.5%\n    - - residual\n      - 12.3%\n      - 5.8%\n      - 38.2%\n    - - G_item\n      - '0.820'\n      - '0.875'\n      - '0.006'\n  - name: 'Table: Methodological Robustness Summary'\n    columns:\n    - Analysis\n    - Result\n    - Conclusion\n    rows:\n    - - REML vs Henderson I\n      - Max diff 0.45pp, all rankings preserved\n      - Henderson I adequate for balanced design\n    - - Fixed vs random (temp/ordering)\n      - Max change 1.8pp\n      - Conclusions robust to facet classification\n    - - Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\n      - Width ratio 0.94–1.06\n      - Independence violation negligible\n    - - Design asymmetry (3×3 vs 2×2)\n      - Δ = 0.7pp on MMLU\n      - Reduced design adequate\n    - - Null simulation (binary)\n      - Obs/null ratio = 526×\n      - Binary G-theory findings are not artifact\n    - - Full-benchmark G projection\n      - G = 0.94–0.99 at full size\n      - Subsampling is the reliability bottleneck\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: Bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id% across 5 benchmarks. Two visual clusters:\n    MC (MMLU, ARC) at 36-48% and non-MC (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) at 19-38%. Endpoint\n    CIs overlap narrowly (MMLU lower vs GSM8K upper), annotated with p=0.003 directional\n    test.\n\n    '\n  - 'D-study projection curves: G vs item count for each benchmark with shaded bootstrap\n    CI bands. Horizontal lines at G=0.80, 0.90. Shows benchmark-specific convergence\n    rates — MMLU crosses 0.80 earliest (~414), MATH latest (~582). IRT recommendation\n    (100-120 items) marked with vertical band showing G=0.41-0.53.\n\n    '\n  - 'Stacked bars: single-model (Llama) vs cross-model (4-model) decomposition for\n    each benchmark. Arrow annotations showing item_id drops (20-25pp) absorbed into\n    model×item. The \"reveal\" figure for Claim 1.\n\n    '\n  - 'Side-by-side: Binary vs logprob vs text-exact scoring metric comparison on MMLU.\n    Three very different stacked bars showing metric-conditional variance structure.\n\n    '\n  - 'Heatmap: model×item interaction strength, 4 models × 5 benchmarks. Mistral shows\n    hot spots on GSM8K (35.2%) and ARC (32.8%); Qwen shows cold spot on GSM8K (11.5%)\n    reflecting superior math capability.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'exp-004 is the analytical backbone of the paper. Five findings for AAAI\n    framing: (1) IRT vs G-theory ratio of 3.6-5.5× across all benchmarks — this is\n    the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" contribution, now confirmed cross-benchmark.\n    (2) Bootstrap CIs show model×item uncertainty is the limiting factor; the paper\n    must honestly report that 4 models provides only rough magnitude estimates (±5pp).\n    (3) D-study budgets are benchmark-conditional with CIs that overlap substantially\n    (MMLU [350,490] vs GSM8K [400,590]) — the practical message is \"400-600 items\n    for any benchmark\" rather than sharply differentiated budgets. (4) Logprob scoring\n    strengthens the metric-conditional narrative: item_id rises from 42% to 55% under\n    continuous scoring, confirming that binary 0/1 compresses item-level information.\n    (5) All methodological robustness checks pass: REML, fixed-vs-random, bootstrap-vs-jackknife,\n    design asymmetry, null simulation. The paper''s Methodological Defense package\n    is complete.\n\n    '\n- name: 14B Model Scale Extension\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'P1 priority. Blocked on exp-003 (descoped) — dependency can be removed\n    if run independently at BF16 only. Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU\n    and GSM8K with reduced design: BF16 × 2 temps × 4 prompts × 4 seeds × 2 orderings\n    × 200 items.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: planned — not started, blocked on hardware availability\n    qwen3_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.752\n    deepseek_r1_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.735\n    qwen3_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.845\n    deepseek_r1_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.812\n    mmlu_item_id_14b_2model: 38.5\n    mmlu_model_x_item_14b: 28.2\n    gsm8k_item_id_14b_2model: 28.5\n    gsm8k_model_x_item_14b: 35.8\n    variance_hierarchy_preserved: true\n    item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: -3.5\n    deepseek_r1_temp_sensitivity_elevated: true\n    deepseek_r1_temp_x_item_gsm8k: 8.5\n  result_rationale: '14B models would show ~3.5pp lower item_id than 8B because higher\n    capacity reduces floor effects — items impossible for 8B become solvable at 14B,\n    compressing between-item variance. model×item rises because dense (Qwen3-14B)\n    vs reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures create larger model-specific\n    item profiles: DeepSeek excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1''s\n    chain-of-thought traces), Qwen excels on factual knowledge. DeepSeek shows elevated\n    temperature×item on GSM8K (~8.5%) because reasoning- distilled models produce\n    branching chain-of-thought that is more sampling- sensitive. Only 2 models limits\n    statistical power — model×item estimates would have very wide CIs. Position as\n    Discussion-section scale validation, not a primary finding. The practical implication:\n    variance hierarchy is stable at 7-14B, suggesting much larger models (70B+) would\n    be needed to alter the structure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: 8B vs 14B Variance Structure (MMLU, BF16)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - 8B (4 models)\n    - 14B (2 models)\n    - Δ\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 42.1%\n      - 38.5%\n      - -3.6pp\n    - - model×item\n      - 33.6%\n      - 28.2%\n      - -5.4pp\n    - - residual\n      - 12.3%\n      - 18.5%\n      - +6.2pp\n    note: 14B model×item appears lower than 8B but is confounded by N=2 models (high\n      residual absorbs signal)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition on MMLU and GSM8K, showing qualitative stability.'\n  analysis: 'Useful for Discussion if compute permits, but not essential for AAAI\n    submission. The key finding (item_id stable, hierarchy preserved) is expected\n    from measurement theory — contribution is confirming the expectation empirically.\n    Low priority given timeline pressure.\n\n    '\n- name: Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 'P1 priority, independent of exp-003. RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA.\n    6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4\n    tasks = 1,728 fine-tuning runs. Validates whether variance hierarchy differs between\n    inference and fine-tuning.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: planned — not started (200 GPU hours, P1)\n    total_fine_tuning_runs: 1728\n    variance_decomposition_summary:\n      SST2:\n        dominant: learning_rate (32%)\n        second: seed (24%)\n        third: data_shuffle (13%)\n        seed_x_lr: 11%\n        gpu: 0.3%\n        precision: 0.8%\n        residual: 18.9%\n      MNLI:\n        dominant: learning_rate (28%)\n        second: seed (22%)\n        third: data_shuffle (16%)\n        seed_x_lr: 12%\n        gpu: 0.4%\n        precision: 0.9%\n        residual: 20.7%\n      RTE:\n        dominant: data_shuffle (26%)\n        second: learning_rate (20%)\n        third: seed×lr (15%)\n        seed: 14%\n        gpu: 0.5%\n        precision: 1.0%\n        residual: 23.5%\n      COPA:\n        dominant: data_shuffle (30%)\n        second: seed (20%)\n        third: learning_rate (16%)\n        seed_x_lr: 12%\n        gpu: 0.4%\n        precision: 1.2%\n        residual: 20.4%\n    hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n    infrastructure_negligible_both_modes: true\n    rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n    rte_bimodal_gap_pp: 4.2\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance is qualitatively different from inference:\n    hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items. On large datasets\n    (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (28-32%) because the optimization landscape\n    is well-defined and LR determines convergence basin. On small datasets (RTE 277\n    examples, COPA 400 examples), data shuffle dominates (26-30%) because mini-batch\n    composition has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single hard\n    example in early batches steers the model into a different loss basin. seed×LR\n    interaction (11-15%) creates bimodal accuracy on RTE: two loss basins separated\n    by ~4pp, consistent with Dodge et al. (2020) and Bui et al. (2025). GPU and precision\n    effects remain negligible (<1.2%) — the infrastructure-is-negligible finding generalizes\n    from inference to fine-tuning. Frame as Discussion-section preliminary finding\n    showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa)\n    may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy'\n    columns:\n    - Rank\n    - Inference (MMLU)\n    - FT-Large (SST-2)\n    - FT-Small (RTE)\n    rows:\n    - - '#1'\n      - item_id (42%)\n      - learning_rate (32%)\n      - data_shuffle (26%)\n    - - '#2'\n      - model×item (34%)\n      - seed (24%)\n      - learning_rate (20%)\n    - - '#3'\n      - residual (12%)\n      - residual (19%)\n      - seed×lr (15%)\n    - - Infra total\n      - precision <0.5%\n      - gpu+prec 1.1%\n      - gpu+prec 1.5%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks), highlighting\n    the qualitative hierarchy shift. Item_id (blue) dominates inference; learning_rate\n    (orange) and data_shuffle (green) dominate fine-tuning.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Downgraded per Dream #3 directive to Discussion-section preliminary observation.\n    The finding that infrastructure is negligible in BOTH inference and fine-tuning\n    strengthens Claim 2, but this is encoder-only and cannot support broad claims.\n    If AAAI timeline is tight, this experiment is the first to cut. Worth doing for\n    ACL ARR October if time permits.\n\n    '\n- name: LayerCast Precision Mitigation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'P2 priority. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional\n    precision condition on MMLU with 4 models.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: planned — not started (30 GPU hours, P2)\n    layercast_accuracy: 0.713\n    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n    precision_x_item_layercast_pct: 0.02\n    inference_overhead_pct: 18\n    g_coefficient_unchanged: true\n  result_rationale: 'LayerCast reduces precision×item from BF16''s already-negligible\n    0.04% (post BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis) to ~0.02% — the improvement is\n    unmeasurable in practice. With BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp from exp-001) and precision\n    contributing <0.1% of variance, any mitigation strategy provides diminishing returns.\n    The 18% inference overhead makes LayerCast cost-ineffective. Position as 1-paragraph\n    appendix material if run; otherwise not worth the GPU hours given AAAI timeline.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Lowest priority. Skip for AAAI unless reviewer explicitly asks about\n    precision mitigation strategies. The BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis from exp-001\n    already shows precision is a non-issue for practical evaluation (0.0006% variance\n    after excluding FP32).\n\n    '\n- name: MATH Benchmark\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: Merged into exp-002 Phase 2. All results included in exp-002 predictions\n    above.\n  expected_results:\n    status: merged_into_exp002\n    see: exp-002 MATH results (3-model preliminary)\n  result_rationale: No standalone experiment — MATH data collected as part of exp-002\n    Phase 2.\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: See exp-002.\n","numLines":1139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1139}}}
