{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VURxBKAbXF2w3TZJjcbzyX","type":"tool_result","content":"# AAAI-26 AI Review Scoring Scale Analysis\n\nBased on my review of the document, **there is no traditional scoring scale or numerical rating system described** for the AAAI-26 AI reviews.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Review Structure:** The AI review system was explicitly designed to exclude scores and recommendations. The document states: \"The AI review system did not include any scores or recommendations, and no human reviewers were replaced in the process.\"\n\n**Survey Dimensions:** Rather than numerical scores, the evaluation used a \"five-point Likert scale\" (-2: strongly disagree through +2: strongly agree) to assess review quality across dimensions including:\n- Overall impressions\n- Review emphasis\n- Technical accuracy\n- Suggestions for research and writing\n\n**Review Content:** AI reviews contained structured elements like \"strengths, weaknesses, and references\" but no scoring mechanism.\n\n## Conclusion:\n\nThe AAAI-26 pilot deliberately avoided creating a scoring rubric for AI-generated reviews, instead integrating them as supplementary input alongside traditional human reviews. This design choice meant assessments focused on qualitative feedback rather than quantitative ratings, distinguishing this approach from conventional conference review processes."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e048c7cb-c26e-4a6a-9302-1c61fd6549ca","uuid":"6f1d662a-6893-44b0-8b89-8c2d79215e0f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:44:59.424Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":252265,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# AAAI-26 AI Review Scoring Scale Analysis\n\nBased on my review of the document, **there is no traditional scoring scale or numerical rating system described** for the AAAI-26 AI reviews.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Review Structure:** The AI review system was explicitly designed to exclude scores and recommendations. The document states: \"The AI review system did not include any scores or recommendations, and no human reviewers were replaced in the process.\"\n\n**Survey Dimensions:** Rather than numerical scores, the evaluation used a \"five-point Likert scale\" (-2: strongly disagree through +2: strongly agree) to assess review quality across dimensions including:\n- Overall impressions\n- Review emphasis\n- Technical accuracy\n- Suggestions for research and writing\n\n**Review Content:** AI reviews contained structured elements like \"strengths, weaknesses, and references\" but no scoring mechanism.\n\n## Conclusion:\n\nThe AAAI-26 pilot deliberately avoided creating a scoring rubric for AI-generated reviews, instead integrating them as supplementary input alongside traditional human reviews. This design choice meant assessments focused on qualitative feedback rather than quantitative ratings, distinguishing this approach from conventional conference review processes.","durationMs":5155,"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.13940v1"}}
